.TH TIGRIS 8 "7 October 2026" "Tigris 3.5.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
tigris \- security auditor for Linux, in POSIX shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tigris
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR profile ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR run ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR tigerrc ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR logdir ]
.RB [ \-e | \-E ]
.RB [ \-H ]
.LP
.B tigris explain
.IR id " ..."
.LP
.B tigris
.RB [ \-v | \-t | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tigris
audits a Linux system: accounts and passwords, file permissions and
setuid files, installed files against the package manager, security
updates waiting, cron, systemd units, sshd and sudo, PAM, the firewall,
kernel and network settings, storage, containers, logging and signs of
intrusion. It writes a text report and, beside it, the same findings as
JSON Lines. It descends from TIGER (Texas A&M University, 1993) and
needs no compiler and no interpreter: shell and awk.
.LP
Run it as root. Run as an ordinary user, the checks that need root are
skipped and listed in the report, rather than guessed at.
.B tigris
runs check scripts only when they belong to the user running it, or,
under sudo, to the user who ran sudo.
.LP
.B tiger
is the same command under its old name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-q
Print nothing while running. The report is still written; with
.BR \-\-since ,
what changed is printed.
.TP
.BI \-p " name" "\fR, \fP" \-\-profile " name"
Read the profile
.I name
on top of the tigerrc: its settings replace the tigerrc's. Profiles are
looked for in a
.I profiles
directory beside the tigerrc, then in Tigris's own:
.B quick
(everything but the file system scan),
.B server
(USB storage expected off; every account's PATH; processes using
deleted files),
.B desktop
(without the checks for what only servers run) and
.B container
(an image or a container: without the host's kernel, boot, firewall,
systemd and disks).
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
Audit the system under
.IR dir ,
which is not running: a disk mounted from another machine, a container
image's root filesystem, a VM snapshot. Its files are read under
.IR dir ,
its symbolic links followed inside it, its files' owners named from its
own passwd and group files, and this host's dpkg, apt, rpm, dnf,
zypper, apk or pacman pointed at its package databases. Nothing under
.I dir
is run. Checks that need the running system, and those that cannot
read an offline root yet, are skipped, and the report lists each with
the reason. Tigris writes nothing under
.IR dir ;
the package tools may write their logs and caches there as they would
on that system, so mount it read-only if it must stay unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " run"
Compare this run with an earlier one and print only what is new and
what was resolved
.RB ( tigris\-diff ).
.I run
is the .jsonl file of an earlier report, or
.B last
for the most recent one of this host in the log directory. The exit
status then counts only the new findings.
.TP
.BI \-c " tigerrc"
Use another control file than
.IR ./tigerrc .
.TP
.BI \-l " logdir"
Write the reports to
.I logdir
instead of
.IR log/ .
.TP
.BI \-w " dir"
Scratch files go to
.I dir
instead of
.IR run/ .
.TP
.BI \-B " dir"
The directory Tigris is installed in.
.TP
.B \-e
Put each finding's explanation in the report after it.
.TP
.B \-E
Write the explanations to a separate report, each once.
.TP
.B \-H
Write the report as HTML, with links to the explanations.
.TP
.B \-G
Generate the signatures (MD5 hashes and permissions) of the system's
binaries.
.TP
.B \-t
Test mode: check the configuration, run nothing.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "explain " id " ..."
Say what each finding id means: its severity, category, the checks that
report it, the compliance controls it is evidence about, and what to do
about it. The same as
.BR tigexp (8).
.SH OUTPUT
The report is
.IR logdir/security.report.HOST.DATE ;
each finding is a line
.BI \-\- LEVEL \-\- " [" id "] " message
with the level ALERT, FAIL, WARN, INFO, ERROR or CONFIG, and a
.B #
line says what each check is doing and what was skipped.
.LP
Beside it,
.I security.report.HOST.DATE.jsonl
holds the same findings as JSON Lines: a
.B run
record first (with the profile and the root, when there were any), one
.B finding
per message with its level, id, check, message, detail, category and
compliance controls,
.B skip
records for the checks that did not run and why, and a
.B summary
with the counts last. INFO findings are always there. The format is a
versioned contract with a JSON Schema; see
.IR doc/json-format.md .
.LP
Each finding maps to the controls it is evidence about in CIS Controls
v8, NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A and UK Cyber
Essentials
.RI ( doc/controls.map ).
A finding is evidence for an assessment, not a verdict on a control.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Nothing at WARN or above.
.TP
.B 1
The audit did not run: a usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 2
A check could not run (an ERROR, or a check skipped because it needs
root), and nothing worse was found.
.TP
.BR 3 ", " 4 ", " 5
The worst finding was a WARN, a FAIL or an ALERT.
.LP
With
.BR \-\-since ,
only the new findings count. Accepted findings
.RB ( tigris\-accept )
do not count.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I tigerrc
Which checks run, and how.
.TP
.I profiles/
Profiles, read on top of the tigerrc.
.TP
.I tigris.accepted
Accepted findings, next to the tigerrc.
.TP
.I meta/
One file per finding id: its severity, category, checks, controls and
explanation.
.TP
.I log/
The reports.
.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
A full audit, then one that skips the file system scan:
.RS
.nf
sudo tigris
sudo tigris \-\-profile quick
.fi
.RE
.LP
Nightly from cron, mailing only when something changed:
.RS
.nf
tigris \-q \-\-since last
.fi
.RE
.LP
A container image, without running it:
.RS
.nf
mkdir rootfs
docker export $(docker create IMAGE) | tar \-C rootfs \-xf \-
sudo tigris \-\-root rootfs
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tigris\-diff (8),
.BR tigris\-accept (8),
.BR tigexp (8),
.BR tigercron (8),
.BR tiger (8)
.LP
https://github.com/creativeheadz/tigris
.SH HISTORY
TIGER was written at Texas A&M University in 1993 by Douglas Lee
Schales, David K. Hess and David R. Safford, and maintained on GNU
Savannah from 2001 to 2019 by Javier Fern\('andez-Sanguino Pe\(~na.
Tigris is an independent fork of it, started in 2026 and maintained by
Andrei Trimbitas. The official TIGER is at
https://savannah.nongnu.org/projects/tiger/.
.SH BUGS
Report them at https://github.com/creativeheadz/tigris/issues;
security issues as SECURITY.md says.
